WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:02.070
- This is deepen our calculus

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.490
about what a good
experience is for a customer

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:07.060
and what's healthy for the future

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:10.230
of our industry and the
future of the world.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.700
- Okay, so stop me if this
isn't the first tech executive

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:13.790
you've heard wax on

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:15.890
about how their product
can save the world.

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.480
Except this isn't just any tech executive.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.650
This is Apple's Craig Federighi.

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:23.040
He sits right below Apple CEO Tim Cook

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.020
on the org chart.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:27.050
Does Tim Cook really call you Superman?

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.960
- Certainly not to my face.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:29.900
- Okay.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.390
Does he call you Hair Force One?

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:32.786
- Lord no.

00:00:32.786 --> 00:00:33.850
(both chuckling)

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:35.090
Usually Craig.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.760
- [Joanna] The software on
your iPhone, iPad or Mac,

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.790
Federighi oversees the
development of it all.

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:42.230
He's got a lot of power

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:44.600
and he's been putting it
behind a new privacy feature

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.010
called ATT, App Tracking Transparency,

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.840
being released today in iOS 14.5.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:54.280
Apps now have to ask to track
you in a popup like this.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.030
Apple only gave me 15
minutes with Mr. Federighi

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:58.320
to talk about the new feature.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.710
- Joanna, am I just a
voice or do I have a face?

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.220
- So I've got my stopwatch.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:05.740
I did not trust the Apple Stopwatch app.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.190
I'm going to give you 30 seconds

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:11.410
to explain what App
Tracking Transparency is.

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:13.730
- All right, I think I can
do it in eight seconds.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:15.210
- And go.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:18.060
- App Tracking Transparency
gives users the choice

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.210
of whether they wanna be tracked
across apps and websites.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.410
- 8.69.

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:24.350
- Oh, sorry.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:25.840
I could do it again faster.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.030
- [Joanna] Well, it's
a bit deeper than that.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:30.800
- It also affects
something called the IDFA,

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.840
which is an identifier that
iOS has had for many years.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.460
- [Joanna] Every iPhone has
this unique tracking number

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:39.580
and it's used to track
your activity between apps.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:41.370
So say you're thinking about buying,

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:43.050
I don't know, Craig's blue shirt

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:44.640
in this shopping app.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.350
The shopping app has your
unique tracking number.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:49.830
Then you open your
favorite social media app

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:50.870
and what do you know?

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:52.030
A shirt ad.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:56.320
That's because the two apps
shared info using the IDFA.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.070
In iOS 14.5, this number
isn't automatically shared.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:01.550
If you wanna let the app share it,

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:03.700
you'd have to tap Allow here.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:04.840
- We needed to make sure both

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.790
that the user had a clear understanding

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:08.660
of the decision

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.510
and that we gave the
developer an opportunity

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:13.980
to explain themselves as to the purpose

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.940
for which they were
potentially asking to track.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.410
And so the developer, of course,

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:22.250
can present messaging even
before they ask the system

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:23.770
to prompt the user.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:25.210
And then they also get a little area

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.520
on the panel itself to explain

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.250
that they're using this for
presenting more effective ads

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:32.630
or whatever the case may be.

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:35.330
- Why the verbiage ask not to track?

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.430
Why not just do not track?

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:38.580
- There are other techniques

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.334
that developers over time have developed,

00:02:41.334 --> 00:02:44.070
like fingerprinting, there's
a bit of cat and mouse game

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:47.140
around other ways that an app might scheme

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:50.400
to create a tracking identifier

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.580
and it's a policy issue for us

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.310
to say you must not do that.

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:58.730
And so we can't ensure at the system level

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:00.730
that they're not tracking.

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:03.290
We can do so at a policy level.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:06.017
- Will there be any Apple
Apps with the ATT popup?

00:03:06.017 --> 00:03:07.590
- No, not at this time.

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:11.620
I mean, there's no Apple
app or service that tracks.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:14.070
There's no app that's sharing information

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:15.440
with external data brokers.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.205
No app or service of ours

00:03:17.205 --> 00:03:20.500
that is tracking you across other vendors,

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.380
apps and websites.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:23.800
If we ever did do something

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.540
that fits the definition of tacking,

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:28.740
we, of course, would
have to show that prompt.

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:31.927
- So I wanted to read a quote
from Steve Jobs back in 2010.

00:03:31.927 --> 00:03:33.380
And he said, "I believe some people."

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:35.470
- Wanna share more data
than other people do.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:36.672
Ask 'em.

00:03:36.672 --> 00:03:38.780
Ask 'em every time.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.170
- We the people of the internet,

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:42.133
we're sort of sick of popups.

00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:44.090
I don't wanna speak for
everyone on the internet

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:45.930
but I think most are.

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:47.290
How did you think about that?

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:50.850
- I share your frustration
with sometimes being bombarded

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:55.150
with every website usually telling you

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:56.510
that they're going to track you

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:57.917
with a big blue Accept button

00:03:57.917 --> 00:03:59.490
and no real choice at all.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:00.970
We had to make it a meaningful choice.

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:02.710
And we had to make it a clear choice

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:03.660
and one that had effect

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.210
and that I think is in deep contrast

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:07.800
to most of what you see on the internet.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.550
As people get today's update

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:11.310
and begin to launch apps,

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:13.490
they'll see it at first and pretty soon,

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:16.500
this will become a pretty
infrequent occurrence,

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.160
both because some apps will stop tracking

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:20.359
or maybe never did.

00:04:20.359 --> 00:04:23.280
And the apps that you use that do track,

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:24.680
you'll make that decision once

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:25.590
and that'll be it.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:26.450
- [Joanna] You also have a choice

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:28.080
to turn off all these popups

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.340
and completely opt out of
any tracking in all apps.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:34.350
You can go to Settings, Privacy, Tracking

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:37.060
and switch off Allows
Apps to Ask to Track.

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:38.210
What will Craig do?

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:40.040
- For me, ask app not to track.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:41.890
- [Joanna] And well, that's
what the industry expects

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:43.540
the majority of people to pick too.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:44.860
And all sorts of companies,

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:46.480
apps and social media networks

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:48.764
that rely on data for
personalized advertising

00:04:48.764 --> 00:04:51.000
aren't happy about it.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.080
Facebook specifically has
called Apple's move hypocritical

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:55.630
and anti-competitive.

00:04:55.630 --> 00:05:00.180
- We've been on this road of
improving privacy protections

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:03.930
in our platform really since
the beginning of iPhone.

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:05.440
Before that truthfully.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:09.810
And so we're accustomed to
initially some panicked responses

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:11.540
from some quarters.

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:13.290
It wasn't surprising to us I think

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:16.220
to hear that some people were
going to push back on this

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:17.053
but at the same thing,

00:05:17.053 --> 00:05:18.910
we were completely confident

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:21.120
that it's the right thing
to give users a choice here.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.860
- But what's in all of this for Apple?

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:26.570
Is this all altruistic for
this trillion dollar company?

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:29.000
How much is privacy factoring
into buying decisions?

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.700
Do you have a sense of that?

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:31.776
- I don't know.

00:05:31.776 --> 00:05:36.760
It's a value that is so deep in us.

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:40.120
Personal information can be used

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:42.040
and abused and even weaponized

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:44.180
in ways that can be
really, really destructive,

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:47.060
often in a way that's not at all apparent

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:50.140
to the person who might be
giving up that information.

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:54.237
Years ago, people were
saying oh, privacy's dead.

00:05:54.237 --> 00:05:56.490
Everyone's given up their privacy.

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:58.900
And so we just kept doing it

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:02.040
because to us, regardless of
whether it was a selling point

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.100
for a phone, we thought
it was the right thing.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:05.410
- A lot of the criticism right now

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:07.730
about Apple is about control.

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:09.320
And that it has too much control,

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:12.450
specifically over its
platforms and the App Store.

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:14.240
What's the conversation like right now

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:17.620
amongst you and other top
brass at Apple about this?

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:18.970
- Well, I think we've always felt

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:22.350
that customers have a real choice

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:25.510
in terms of what phone they wanna buy,

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:28.540
what ecosystem they wanna be a part of.

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:31.480
And we think we're
offering people a choice

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:34.640
to buy into an ecosystem

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.700
where there is a degree of curation,

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:38.680
where there is protection for privacy,

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:40.550
where there are certain standards.

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:43.140
We don't think everyone's
going to prefer that choice.

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:45.405
- Do you feel like there will need

00:06:45.405 --> 00:06:48.365
to be from Apple a lessening
of some of the control?

00:06:48.365 --> 00:06:49.240
- I don't know.

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:51.420
I feel like we're making
the right choices now.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:54.230
We're always considering
the right things primarily

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:56.410
from a customer perspective.

00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:58.040
So as long as we're keeping our eye

00:06:58.040 --> 00:06:59.990
on that North Star,

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:03.730
I think we feel good
about the course we're on.

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:04.563
- And of course,

00:07:04.563 --> 00:07:06.710
I had to sneak in a few
non-privacy questions.

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:08.820
Wireless charging, corded charging?

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:11.470
- I am primarily a cord guys.

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:12.730
I have enough devices

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:16.270
that it works out for with
my stacking arrangements.

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:18.930
- Tim said, I believe it
was a couple weeks ago,

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:21.220
that he says that he won't
be at Apple for at least

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:23.160
in the next 10 years, he
will not stay at Apple

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:24.504
for the next 10 years.

00:07:24.504 --> 00:07:27.410
Your name's being floating.

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:28.313
- Insanity.

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:32.180
Yeah, no, that would be irresponsible.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:34.928
- How's the software going
in the mixed reality headset?

00:07:34.928 --> 00:07:37.496
- I don't know anything about
what you're talking about.

00:07:37.496 --> 00:07:38.329
- What do you think of mixed reality?

00:07:38.329 --> 00:07:39.730
- I think it's got a lot of potential.

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:41.150
I think it deserves exploration.

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:43.430
- [Joanna] Whatever that next
big Apple product looks like,

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:45.730
it's a pretty safe bet that Mr. Federighi

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:48.430
is gonna be pushing
hard for privacy in it.

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:51.720
- These devices are so intimately a part

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.080
of our lives and contain so much

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:56.300
of what we're thinking
and where we've been

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:57.400
and who we've been with

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:58.790
that users deserve

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:00.950
and need control of that information.

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:03.403
Abuses can range from creepy to dangerous.

